    "available_at": "2026-10-12T19:35:22.692848+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nThis Is a One-Owner 1964 1/2 Mustang Convertible, and Fate Has Been So Cruel to It\n114 photos\nPhoto: Flashback Enterprises Inc on eBay\nA mix of good news and bad news. That’s what you’ll be getting if you stick around for a few more minutes to discover this 1964 1/2 Mustang convertible. The car is here because it needs a second chance, begging for restoration on eBay, where the owner listed it for sale.\nThe owner is realistic and admits that in addition to serving as a restoration project, this Mustang is so rough that it can also be used for parts. Despite being a 1964 1/2 Mustang, all parts would also fit a 1965 sibling.\nIn fact, the 1964 1/2 Mustangs don’t exist officially. They are just early 1965 models, so except for generators and alternators, the two models are identical mechanical-wise. Ford operated some changes in the engine lineup when the 1965 model year officially came to be, introducing a new six and replacing the base V8 choice.\nIt’s no secret that the Mustang was a hit from the first day of availability.Ford received no more, no less than 22,000 orders on the first day alone, so when the 1965 Mustang was released officially in mid-August, the carmaker had already received 120,000 cars.\nThe Mustang wasn’t necessarily an expensive car, and the base hardtop, which was fitted with a six-cylinder engine and a 3-speed manual transmission, could be had for only $2,320. The convertible was a bit more expensive at $2,557. For eventually launched a 2+2 fastback in September to help the entire lineup reach final sales of 680,989 units in its first year on the market.\nThe Mustang was originally sold with a 170ci six-cylinder unit rated at 105 horsepower. This engine was dropped when the 1965 Mustang was officially ready. A 200ci six with 120 horsepower became the standard unit on six-cylinder configurations.\nThe base V8 on 1964 1/2 Mustangs was a 260ci unit with a two-barrel configuration. This engine also got the ax when the 1965 model year received the green light, and a 289 with a two-barrel carburetor became the base V8 with 200 horsepower.\nA four-barrel version - which was also offered on early models, but with upgraded power now - was available as an option with 225 horsepower. The top choice was the HiPo configuration that produced over 270 horsepower.\nThis Mustang is fitted with a 289ci engine and an automatic transmission. This should, at least theoretically, be good news for someone who wants to restore the car to day-one specifications without losing the original goodies.\nThis is a one-owner car, and considering so many documents are available, you should be able to check all numbers quite easily. This means it shouldn’t take long to figure out if the engine is original, and for a restoration guy, this is big news.\nOn the other hand, the engine isn’t by any means in good shape. “The engine is stuck from sitting,” the seller says, unfortunately without sharing more specifics. There’s a chance this car has been parked for decades, possibly even outside, judging from the rust damage we can see in the pictures.\nWhile I’ve seen cars in worse shape getting a second chance, I believe this Mustang is worth an in-person inspection. There’s a lot of rust damage on the cars, and hats off to the seller for posting such a large photo gallery.\nI don’t believe this Mustang should be used for parts for a very simple reason: it’s a one-owner specimen that still has the original documents, including the original title, loan approval, and bill of sale. These make the Mustang more of a collectible, despite not being exactly a rare car.\nThe convertible body style is also a solid selling point because most of the cars that were sold initially were hardtops. The convertible top still works and goes up and down, but the seller says it comes with a few holes caused by mice.\nThe selling price makes perfect sense for this car. The owner is realistic and knows that saving this Mustang not only will involve a lot of work but will also eat up more money, especially in terms of metalwork.\nThe engine also isn’t working, so anyone who wants to save it for the sake of keeping the car original will have to spend additional dollars to get it up and running.\nThat is why $5,000 looks like a fair price. However, the owner also enabled the Make Offer option on eBay, meaning that buyers who want the car but don’t want to pay that much can easily send their offers through the auction platform. The listing expires in just one day, but there is a chance that the Mustang will be relisted in the coming weeks if it fails to find a new home.\nThe car is parked in Imlay City, Michigan, and will obviously need transportation to your garage.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMustang Ford Ford Mustang mustang convertible\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT The BMW M6 Convertible Now Costs Virtually Nothing in the States",
